hooke-spring-answer-why = A spring stretches because the particles of its metal move a little apart, and the forces between them pull them back. For small shifts these forces grow in proportion to the shift — in every coil, so the whole spring lengthens in proportion to the force: twice the load, twice the extension. The ratio F/x — the stiffness — depends on the spring: the wire’s thickness, the diameter and the number of coils. This holds while the deformation is elastic: a spring stretched too far does not return to its length — Hooke’s law does not hold there.
hooke-spring-answer-say = The extension is proportional to the force: k ≈ { $kMean } N/m!
hooke-spring-answer-text = { $n } × 100 g: x = { $x }
hooke-spring-answer-text-2 = Each 100 g weight pulls the spring with ≈ 0.98 N. { $steps }. As many times as the force grows, so does the extension, and F/x stays nearly the same: k ≈ { $kMean } N/m (a spread of { $spread } % — the ruler shows only millimetres). That is Hooke’s law: F = k·x. It holds while the deformation is elastic — take the weights off, and the spring comes back to { $l0 }. The textbook does this lab work with a rubber cord; rubber follows Hooke’s law less closely than a steel spring, especially when stretched far.
